Author: thesa <thesa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 7 14:06:57 2026 -0400

    fix(trade_cycle): STRONG/MED tier now requires validate()'s edge gates too
    
    trade_cycle.py classified candidates into TIER_STRONG/TIER_MED using only
    abs(adjusted_edge) vs eff_strong_edge/MED_EDGE, while order_executor.py's
    _validate_trade_opportunity (the gate every candidate must clear immediately
    before placement) independently required net_edge > 0 and raw edge
    (blended_prob - market_prob, side-agnostic) to agree in sign with
    recommended_side and clear MIN_EDGE in magnitude. Because tier classification
    never checked either, some fraction of every cycle's announced STRONG/MED
    signals were structurally unable to place regardless of price/timing --
    confirmed live: KXHIGHTSEA-26AUG07-T85 was announced STRONG (net_edge/
    adjusted_edge +0.335) but could never have placed (raw edge -0.1456, never
    clearing MIN_EDGE).
    
    Fix mirrors the deterministic subset of validate()'s edge gates into tier
    classification: net_edge must be positive, and raw edge must clear the same
    sign/magnitude check validate() applies. A new dbg["placement_gate"] counter
    and cron.py filter-breakdown field report how many otherwise-qualifying
    candidates this excludes. Deliberately NOT mirrored (documented in code and
    a new follow-up backlog entry): the Kelly floor (blocked by a suite-wide
    test-fixture audit -- many existing STRONG/MED-tier fixtures never set
    kelly, defaulting to 0.0) and the confidence-tiered/A-B-test min_edge check
    (provably subsumed at current env values, but fragile to a future MED_EDGE
    change).
    
    9 new tests including a cross-check class that runs the same analysis dict
    through both run_trade_cycle() and the real _validate_trade_opportunity(),
    asserting a tiered candidate always clears validate()'s own edge gates --
    added so future drift between the two is caught automatically rather than
    requiring four one-directional assertions to individually go stale.
    
    Independent opus review: 1 HIGH (first draft missed validate()'s net_edge<=0
    check, mirrored only the raw-edge block), 3 MEDIUM (Kelly floor deferral now
    documented; MIN_EDGE moved to a function-local import matching
    order_executor's own pattern, avoiding staleness after a live Settings-screen
    edit; corrected an inaccurate "stateful" rationale for skipping the
    confidence-tier/AB-test gate to the real numeric-domination argument), 4 LOW
    (missing side="no" test coverage reproducing the actual incident shape;
    missing cross-check test; stale docstring; missing dbg counter) all fixed or
    explicitly deferred with reasoning recorded in backlog.txt.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

